1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Siberian hunters saw — a hunt
that never ends.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
In central Siberia, its Russian name is
Los’: the moose. Farther east, mostly a
bear.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
For the Evenki, the sky is an upper
forest. There lives a cosmic moose:
Kheglen.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Each evening it carries off the sun on
its antlers. Night falls.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
The hunter Mangi follows on skis, shoots
the moose and takes the sun back. Day
breaks.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The bowl’s four stars: the moose’s legs.
The handle’s three: the hunters. Every
night, it happens again.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The Little Dipper: a calf following its
mother. The Milky Way: Mangi’s ski
track.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Some scholars think the story crossed
the Bering land bridge with people, over
10,000 years ago.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The Mi’kmaq of North America tell it
too: the bowl is a bear, the handle its
hunters.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
The whole sky turns around the Pole
Star.

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Bowl first, handle behind: the hunters
chase the moose forever. In the Evenki
forest, it never sets.

12
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
After dark, the moose climbs in the
northeast, hunters behind. They circle
the Pole Star all night.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Sámi: a reindeer hunt across the sky.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

